# Line endings are pinned to LF so generated artifacts are byte-identical across
# platforms. Without an explicit rule, core.autocrlf=true (common on Windows) checks
# text files out as CRLF; anything Chock then regenerates with LF is reported
# as modified by `git status` while `git diff` shows nothing -- noise that trains
# people to run `git checkout --` over generated output.
#
# Pair this with chock.emit.write_generated(), which writes LF regardless of
# platform. Both halves are required: the attribute governs checkout, the writer
# governs regeneration.

.git/hooks/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.tmpl text eol=lf
chock.lock text eol=lf

# Generated trees: every file, including extensionless shims.
.chock/** text eol=lf

# Packaged hook scripts are extensionless, so the *.sh rule above does not reach them.
# A CRLF checkout on Windows gets built into the wheel and then fails on Linux with
# `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M` -- on the adopter's machine, not ours.
src/chock/hooks/data/** text eol=lf
src/chock/packs/**/implementations/** text eol=lf

# Cursor hook config is generated (chock install-hooks) and may be committed; the
# baked interpreter is machine-specific but entry identity is interpreter-normalised.
.cursor/hooks.json text eol=lf
